  "organic reaction basics": {
    highlights: [
      "Curved Arrow Notation & Bond Cleavage: Curved arrows depict pair electron movement; fishhook arrows depict single electron movement; Homolytic cleavage yields neutral Free Radicals; Heterolytic cleavage yields charged Carbocations (carbonium ions) and Carbanions.",
      "Reactive Intermediates (Carbocations): sp^2 hybridized carbon with vacant p-orbital; planar geometry; stability governed by hyperconjugation and inductive effect: \\text{tertiary} (3^\\circ) > \\text{secondary} (2^\\circ) > \\text{primary} (1^\\circ) > \\text{methyl}; allylic and benzylic carbocations stabilized by resonance.",
      "Reactive Intermediates (Carbanions & Radicals): Carbanions: sp^3 hybridized with lone pair, pyramidal geometry, stability inverted: \\text{methyl} > 1^\\circ > 2^\\circ > 3^\\circ (electron-withdrawing groups stabilize); Free Radicals: sp^2 hybridized with unpaired electron, stability order: 3^\\circ > 2^\\circ > 1^\\circ > \\text{methyl}.",
      "Inductive Effect (I-Effect): Permanent polarization transmitted along \\sigma-bonds due to electronegativity differences; diminishes rapidly after 3 bonds; +I groups (electron releasing: -C(CH_3)_3 > -CH(CH_3)_2 > -CH_2CH_3 > -CH_3); -I groups (electron withdrawing: -NO_2 > -CN > -COOH > -F > -Cl > -Br > -I).",
      "Electromeric Effect (E-Effect): Temporary polarizability displacement of \\pi-electrons in presence of an attacking reagent; +E effect (electrons transfer toward reagent); -E effect (electrons transfer away from reagent).",
      "Resonance / Mesomeric Effect (M-Effect): Delocalization of \\pi-electrons through conjugated systems; +M groups donate lone pair into ring (-OH, -NH_2, -OCH_3, ortho/para directing, activating); -M groups withdraw electron density (-NO_2, -CHO, -COOH, meta directing, deactivating).",
      "Hyperconjugation (Baker-Nathan Effect): Delocalization of \\sigma-electrons of C-H bonds with adjacent empty or partially filled p-orbital ('no-bond resonance'); stability of carbocations and alkenes increases directly with number of \\alpha-hydrogens.",
      "Aromaticity & Hückel's Rule (4n + 2 \\pi): Planar, cyclic, fully conjugated system possessing (4n + 2) \\pi-electrons (n = 0, 1, 2..) possesses exceptional thermodynamic stability (e.g. Benzene 6 \\pi, Cyclopentadienyl anion 6 \\pi, Tropylium cation 6 \\pi); Anti-aromatic systems possess 4n \\pi-electrons and are exceptionally unstable (Cyclobutadiene 4 \\pi).",
      "Electrophiles & Nucleophiles: Electrophiles (electron-deficient Lewis acids seeking electrons: H^+, NO_2^+, AlCl_3, BF_3); Nucleophiles (electron-rich Lewis bases with lone pairs or negative charge: OH^-, CN^-, NH_3, H_2O, carbanions).",
      "Types of Organic Reactions: Substitution (S_N1, S_N2, S_EAr), Addition (electrophilic addition to alkenes, nucleophilic addition to carbonyls), Elimination (E1, E2, E1cB), and Molecular Rearrangements (Wagner-Meerwein carbocation shift).",
    ],
    keyPoints: [
      "Carbocation Stability Hierarchy: 3° > 2° > 1° > Methyl (stabilized by hyperconjugation and +I effect).",
      "Carbanion Stability Hierarchy: Methyl > 1° > 2° > 3° (destabilized by +I alkyl groups).",
      "Free Radical Stability: 3° > 2° > 1° > Methyl (mirrors carbocations).",
      "Hückel's Rule for Aromaticity: Planar, cyclic, conjugated with (4n + 2) \\pi-electrons.",
      "Anti-Aromatic Definition: Cyclic, planar, conjugated with 4n \\pi-electrons (highly destabilized).",
      "Hyperconjugation Indicator: Count number of \\alpha-hydrogens attached to carbon adjacent to carbocation or double bond.",
      "Homolytic Cleavage Product: Two uncharged free radicals (single-electron movement).",
      "Heterolytic Cleavage Product: Anion and cation (two-electron movement).",
      "+M Substituent Orientation: Ortho- and Para-directing on benzene ring.",
      "-M Substituent Orientation: Meta-directing on benzene ring.",
    ],
  },

